\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Flux in more than one plane}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{In dimension #{2m\ge4} the Wilson term adds the contributions of the planes. For independent scalar fields on disjoint coordinate planes, \ref{fdip-01CB} expresses the half-signature through their oriented fluxes. A field coupling different planes still has plaquette fluxes on two-dimensional surfaces. What quantity on a full #{2m}-cycle could replace the product of plane fluxes for such a field?}
